1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,214
This is a technical walkthrough of a white paper by Edmond Ying, titled The Quantized Space-Ledger:

2
00:00:06,214 --> 00:00:10,231
Locating Quantum Non-Locality in a Discrete Spacetime Substrate.

3
00:00:10,231 --> 00:00:15,252
This video follows the current, revised seventy-three page version of the paper.

4
00:00:15,252 --> 00:00:18,077
The paper is explicit that it is speculative.

5
00:00:18,077 --> 00:00:23,789
It offers a potential candidate hypothesis, not an established result, and this video keeps

6
00:00:23,789 --> 00:00:25,295
that framing throughout.

7
00:00:25,995 --> 00:00:31,009
Because the audience here may be skeptical, we will separate two things carefully.

8
00:00:31,009 --> 00:00:36,451
Standard physics, meaning results that are measured or derived in mainstream theory, will

9
00:00:36,451 --> 00:00:42,321
be labeled as such. The ledger proposal, meaning the paper's own reinterpretation or hypothesis,

10
00:00:42,321 --> 00:00:47,763
will be labeled as a proposal. Where the paper says something is an open problem, we will

11
00:00:47,763 --> 00:00:48,435
say so too.

12
00:00:49,135 --> 00:00:55,324
We will cover twelve chapters: the client and server picture, now with a finiteness principle;

13
00:00:55,324 --> 00:01:00,525
the photoelectric effect; mass as frequency, with a candidate hardware picture;

14
00:01:00,525 --> 00:01:05,463
measurement as queries, with numerical tests of alternative read-out rules;

15
00:01:05,463 --> 00:01:09,018
entanglement as a shared record, with a toy Bell test;

16
00:01:09,018 --> 00:01:13,561
the information cap per node; the Higgs field versus the Higgs boson;

17
00:01:13,561 --> 00:01:16,326
a toy lattice study of the critical point;

18
00:01:16,326 --> 00:01:20,144
steps toward gravity, including light bending and Mercury;

19
00:01:20,144 --> 00:01:23,831
black holes without singularities; testable predictions;

20
00:01:23,831 --> 00:01:25,280
and the open problems.

21
00:01:25,979 --> 00:01:28,931
Chapter one. The Client–Server Picture.

22
00:01:29,631 --> 00:01:32,594
The paper's single organizing hypothesis is this:

23
00:01:32,594 --> 00:01:38,277
spacetime itself is a discrete, entangled network of quantized nodes, called the Space-Ledger,

24
00:01:38,277 --> 00:01:40,877
and that network carries the quantum state.

25
00:01:40,877 --> 00:01:46,076
In other words, the wavefunction is assigned to spacetime rather than to the particle.

26
00:01:46,076 --> 00:01:51,336
What we call a particle is the localized excitation that is rendered on the client side

27
00:01:51,336 --> 00:01:55,024
when the ledger is queried by a measurement-like interaction.

28
00:01:55,723 --> 00:02:00,414
The framework uses two layers. The server side is spacetime, the ledger.

29
00:02:00,414 --> 00:02:06,343
It holds the global quantum state, the phases, the entanglement, and the Bell correlations,

30
00:02:06,343 --> 00:02:10,903
and it operates at a scale approaching zero length, the Planck regime.

31
00:02:10,903 --> 00:02:13,705
The client side is the observable universe:

32
00:02:13,705 --> 00:02:17,027
matter, signals, detectors, records, and observers.

33
00:02:17,027 --> 00:02:22,825
It is local. Nothing on the client side propagates faster than light, massive excitations

34
00:02:22,825 --> 00:02:26,865
move slower than light, and massless ones move at light speed.

35
00:02:26,865 --> 00:02:32,012
The paper stresses that this analogy is a guide to intuition, not a derivation.

36
00:02:32,711 --> 00:02:36,843
Why assume discrete nodes at all? The paper cites three lines of standard physics that

37
00:02:36,843 --> 00:02:39,246
hint in that direction. First, black-hole entropy:

38
00:02:39,246 --> 00:02:43,474
the Bekenstein-Hawking result, S equals k A over four l P squared, read as a bound, caps

39
00:02:43,474 --> 00:02:45,828
the information in a region by its boundary area.

40
00:02:45,828 --> 00:02:50,056
Second, ultraviolet divergences in quantum field theory, which a physical minimum length

41
00:02:50,056 --> 00:02:52,602
would cut off naturally. Third, an operational limit:

42
00:02:52,602 --> 00:02:56,734
probing below roughly the Planck length would take so much energy in so small a region

43
00:02:56,734 --> 00:02:58,271
that it would form a black hole.

44
00:02:58,971 --> 00:03:03,259
The revised paper adds a working principle, labeled a hypothesis:

45
00:03:03,259 --> 00:03:08,337
the finiteness principle. No physical quantity in the model becomes infinite.

46
00:03:08,337 --> 00:03:14,537
First, nodes have a fixed size of about one Planck length, so no physical distance is shorter.

47
00:03:14,537 --> 00:03:21,132
Second, each node carries a finite workload, capped, in the recommended form, by a frame-independent

48
00:03:21,132 --> 00:03:27,002
limiting curvature. So curvature, density, and collision energy per node all have maximum

49
00:03:27,002 --> 00:03:32,872
values. Candidate consequences: a finite core in place of the black-hole singularity, and

50
00:03:32,872 --> 00:03:35,840
a natural cutoff for ultraviolet divergences.

51
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:41,710
The cap value is a placeholder, not derived, and a cutoff alone does not preserve Lorentz

52
00:03:41,710 --> 00:03:42,435
invariance.

53
00:03:43,135 --> 00:03:46,156
Here the revised paper changes its emphasis.

54
00:03:46,156 --> 00:03:50,482
A regular grid picks out preferred directions and a rest frame.

55
00:03:50,482 --> 00:03:56,936
The paper now keeps an organized, body-centred-cubic grid, and argues that sums over link-only

56
00:03:56,936 --> 00:04:03,253
hop paths leave a direction dependence of the speed of light only at order E over the Planck

57
00:04:03,253 --> 00:04:08,402
energy, squared. On this cubic test grid, the hop-path front becomes round:

58
00:04:08,402 --> 00:04:12,110
a diagonal to axis radius ratio of 0.997 at 120 ticks.

59
00:04:12,110 --> 00:04:18,151
A random, causal-set-like network is kept as a fallback if boost invariance requires it.

60
00:04:18,851 --> 00:04:24,079
On the network, the ledger updates in discrete ticks of roughly the Planck time, on nodes

61
00:04:24,079 --> 00:04:29,307
spaced roughly the Planck length apart. Because the update couples only neighboring nodes

62
00:04:29,307 --> 00:04:32,538
per tick, nothing advances more than one node per tick.

63
00:04:32,538 --> 00:04:37,942
That gives a client-side speed limit of c equals a over tau, which matches the Planck length

64
00:04:37,942 --> 00:04:40,115
over the Planck time by construction.

65
00:04:40,815 --> 00:04:46,398
Bell tests, which agree with quantum mechanics, show that any framework reproducing quantum

66
00:04:46,398 --> 00:04:49,834
predictions must be non-local in Bell's sense somewhere.

67
00:04:49,834 --> 00:04:52,779
The ledger does not eliminate that non-locality.

68
00:04:52,779 --> 00:04:56,337
It locates it: an entangled pair is one server-side state.

69
00:04:56,337 --> 00:05:01,736
Each client-side read looks locally random, so the no-signaling condition holds, and the

70
00:05:01,736 --> 00:05:07,319
correlation becomes visible only when records are compared over an ordinary channel limited

71
00:05:07,319 --> 00:05:09,895
by c. The paper notes a remaining tension:

72
00:05:09,895 --> 00:05:15,294
if the read-out is a physical update, it needs an ordering of spacelike-separated events

73
00:05:15,294 --> 00:05:17,871
that clients cannot observe. That is open.

74
00:05:17,871 --> 00:05:20,631
Chapter five returns to this with a toy test.

75
00:05:21,331 --> 00:05:24,235
Chapter two. Light in Quantized Packets.

76
00:05:24,935 --> 00:05:27,701
This chapter is standard physics background.

77
00:05:27,701 --> 00:05:31,033
When light strikes a metal, electrons can be ejected.

78
00:05:31,033 --> 00:05:36,815
The classic observation is that whether electrons come out depends on the light's frequency,

79
00:05:36,815 --> 00:05:42,221
not on its brightness. Below a threshold frequency, no electrons are emitted no matter

80
00:05:42,221 --> 00:05:47,815
how intense the light. Above it, electrons appear immediately, even at low intensity, and

81
00:05:47,815 --> 00:05:51,335
brighter light produces more electrons, not faster ones.

82
00:05:52,036 --> 00:05:57,517
How does this connect to the ledger? In the paper, a particle is not an autonomous object

83
00:05:57,517 --> 00:06:02,383
sliding across a continuous plane; it is a localized excitation of the network.

84
00:06:02,383 --> 00:06:07,679
On that reading, a photon arriving at the metal is an excitation of the ledger that is

85
00:06:07,679 --> 00:06:13,222
registered, or rendered, as a single localized quantum when it interacts with an electron.

86
00:06:13,222 --> 00:06:17,410
To be clear, this is an interpretation of the same standard physics.

87
00:06:17,410 --> 00:06:20,860
It changes no prediction about the photoelectric effect.

88
00:06:21,560 --> 00:06:24,055
Chapter three. Mass as Frequency.

89
00:06:24,756 --> 00:06:31,069
Combine two standard relations. Rest energy is E equals m c squared, and quantum energy

90
00:06:31,069 --> 00:06:37,672
is E equals h f. Setting them equal gives each massive particle a characteristic frequency,

91
00:06:37,672 --> 00:06:41,518
the Compton frequency, f C equals m c squared over h.

92
00:06:41,518 --> 00:06:47,759
This is standard physics. The paper's step is to read rest mass, in ledger terms, as a

93
00:06:47,759 --> 00:06:52,475
characteristic frequency of an excitation pattern on the network.

94
00:06:53,176 --> 00:06:56,382
The paper also gives mass a role in propagation.

95
00:06:56,382 --> 00:07:00,390
In client-server language, mass is extra per-hop processing.

96
00:07:00,390 --> 00:07:06,202
At every tick, a massive excitation has some amplitude to flip its direction of travel,

97
00:07:06,202 --> 00:07:09,075
its chirality, instead of simply moving on.

98
00:07:09,075 --> 00:07:15,087
This follows Feynman's checkerboard model and the Dirac quantum walks and quantum cellular

99
00:07:15,087 --> 00:07:20,899
automata in the literature. A massless excitation hops one node per tick and travels at

100
00:07:20,899 --> 00:07:27,112
exactly c. A massive one zigzags, so each hop is still at c, but the average drift is slower.

101
00:07:27,812 --> 00:07:33,972
In one spatial dimension, the excitation is a two-component amplitude, right-moving and

102
00:07:33,972 --> 00:07:40,275
left-moving. One tick mixes them with an angle theta, where sine theta equals m c squared

103
00:07:40,275 --> 00:07:46,436
tau over h-bar. The exact lattice dispersion relation is cosine omega tau equals cosine

104
00:07:46,436 --> 00:07:52,526
theta times cosine k a. In the continuum limit this becomes the Dirac equation, with E

105
00:07:52,526 --> 00:07:58,828
squared equals p squared c squared plus m squared c to the fourth, and the group velocity

106
00:07:58,828 --> 00:08:03,644
is c times the square root of one minus m c squared over E, squared.

107
00:08:04,344 --> 00:08:10,395
The revised paper also offers one candidate hardware picture, explicitly not a claim about

108
00:08:10,395 --> 00:08:16,177
how the server is built. Each node is an identical core, fixed on a body-centred-cubic

109
00:08:16,177 --> 00:08:22,094
lattice with eight neighbours. At every tick, cores act at once on non-overlapping pairs

110
00:08:22,094 --> 00:08:28,078
in a checkerboard, or Margolus, pattern, so an amplitude moves at most one core per tick.

111
00:08:28,078 --> 00:08:34,196
In a one-dimensional run of 300 ticks, probability outside the light cone was exactly zero,

112
00:08:34,196 --> 00:08:40,315
packet speeds matched the dispersion formula to within 0.013, and probability was conserved

113
00:08:40,315 --> 00:08:46,232
to about ten to the minus thirteen. Naive in-place updates, by contrast, wipe data, leak

114
00:08:46,232 --> 00:08:48,720
probability, or break the light cone.

115
00:08:49,420 --> 00:08:50,072
So speed depends on mass relative to energy, not on mass alone.

116
00:08:50,072 --> 00:08:50,797
The more of an excitation's energy is rest energy, the slower it runs.

117
00:08:50,797 --> 00:08:51,719
As energy greatly exceeds m c squared, speed approaches c, and when E equals m c squared,

118
00:08:51,719 --> 00:08:52,610
the excitation is at rest. Special relativity then emerges at low energies, but is not

119
00:08:52,610 --> 00:08:53,501
built in exactly; deviations would appear at order E over the Planck energy or higher,

120
00:08:53,501 --> 00:08:53,740
depending on the graph.

121
00:08:54,440 --> 00:08:57,731
The paper then raises a speculative hypothesis.

122
00:08:57,731 --> 00:09:03,895
Matter fermions come in three generations that share charges and interactions and differ

123
00:09:03,895 --> 00:09:09,919
only in mass. The paper proposes that the generations may be three resonance modes, or

124
00:09:09,919 --> 00:09:16,082
harmonics, of one excitation pattern. For the charged leptons, the muon-to-electron mass

125
00:09:16,082 --> 00:09:22,736
ratio is about 206.77, and the tau-to-electron ratio about 3477.2, so their Compton frequencies

126
00:09:22,736 --> 00:09:28,760
stand in the same ratios. The empirical Koide relation is an example of a pattern such

127
00:09:28,760 --> 00:09:34,784
a model would need to reproduce. Neither the Standard Model nor the paper explains it.

128
00:09:35,484 --> 00:09:38,423
Neutrinos show frequency structure directly.

129
00:09:38,423 --> 00:09:44,036
Each flavor state is a superposition of three mass states, mixed by the PMNS matrix.

130
00:09:44,036 --> 00:09:50,450
The phases of the mass components advance at rates that differ in proportion to the mass-squared

131
00:09:50,450 --> 00:09:56,463
differences, so the flavor composition oscillates with distance L and energy E, with phase

132
00:09:56,463 --> 00:09:59,537
proportional to delta m squared L over four E.

133
00:09:59,537 --> 00:10:05,483
This was established by Super-Kamiokande and SNO, and recognized by the 2015 Nobel Prize.

134
00:10:05,483 --> 00:10:10,427
The curve on screen is an illustrative two-flavor sketch, not fitted data.

135
00:10:11,128 --> 00:10:15,383
Everything about neutrino oscillation just described is standard physics.

136
00:10:15,383 --> 00:10:18,881
The harmonic picture reinterprets it and does not derive it.

137
00:10:18,881 --> 00:10:23,953
The model does not currently predict the mass-squared splittings, the mixing angles, or

138
00:10:23,953 --> 00:10:29,141
the absolute neutrino mass scale, and it would gain real support only if those quantities

139
00:10:29,141 --> 00:10:31,648
followed from the network's mode structure.

140
00:10:32,348 --> 00:10:34,988
Chapter four. Measurement as Queries.

141
00:10:35,688 --> 00:10:40,778
Start with the double slit. A which-way detector at one slit couples detector states to

142
00:10:40,778 --> 00:10:46,221
the paths. Because the detector states are orthogonal, the cross term at the screen vanishes,

143
00:10:46,221 --> 00:10:51,312
and the fringes disappear. This is standard quantum mechanics written in node language.

144
00:10:52,012 --> 00:10:57,499
The paper splits collapse into two steps, and is explicit about which is explained and

145
00:10:57,499 --> 00:11:00,370
which is postulated. Step one is decoherence.

146
00:11:00,370 --> 00:11:06,049
The detector quickly entangles with many environment nodes, and the reduced state becomes

147
00:11:06,049 --> 00:11:11,217
effectively diagonal. In ledger language, the phase correlation is not destroyed.

148
00:11:11,217 --> 00:11:17,024
It is spread into a huge number of environment nodes, where no local client can practically

149
00:11:17,024 --> 00:11:20,980
gather it back. This is standard decoherence theory, restated.

150
00:11:21,680 --> 00:11:24,790
Step two is the read-out, and it is a postulate.

151
00:11:24,790 --> 00:11:30,492
Decoherence explains why alternatives stop interfering, but not why one definite outcome

152
00:11:30,492 --> 00:11:36,323
occurs. The read-out postulate states: when a client-side system extracts information from

153
00:11:36,323 --> 00:11:42,090
the ledger, creating an irreversible, decohered record, exactly one outcome k is sampled,

154
00:11:42,090 --> 00:11:47,792
locked, and returned to the client side, with probability equal to the squared magnitude

155
00:11:47,792 --> 00:11:52,328
of c k. That is, the Born rule is taken as the ledger's read-out rule.

156
00:11:53,028 --> 00:11:58,377
The paper stresses that this is a postulate, not a derivation, and calls deriving it the

157
00:11:58,377 --> 00:12:02,025
main open problem. The revised version narrows that problem.

158
00:12:02,025 --> 00:12:07,374
Gleason's theorem shows that, given a Hilbert-space structure and additive outcomes, the

159
00:12:07,374 --> 00:12:12,967
square is the only consistent read-out. The remaining question is why server-side amplitudes

160
00:12:12,967 --> 00:12:18,985
behave like rotatable arrows, that is, why the ledger's state space carries a complex Hilbert-space

161
00:12:18,985 --> 00:12:24,821
structure with unitary evolution. It is also open whether the read-out is a physical server-side

162
00:12:24,821 --> 00:12:27,132
update or only a client-relative fact.

163
00:12:27,831 --> 00:12:32,656
The paper also tests alternatives numerically, with illustrative parameters:

164
00:12:32,656 --> 00:12:38,624
Sorkin's three-slit parameter kappa against measured bounds, merging detectors, and signalling

165
00:12:38,624 --> 00:12:44,084
through an entangled pair. Power rules give kappa of about five times ten to the minus

166
00:12:44,084 --> 00:12:48,147
three to four times ten to the minus two, exceeding both bounds.

167
00:12:48,147 --> 00:12:51,385
Softmax and exponential rules fail all three tests.

168
00:12:51,385 --> 00:12:54,178
Only the squared amplitude passes all three.

169
00:12:54,178 --> 00:12:59,701
This assumes additivity of the read-out, which a concrete ledger model would still have

170
00:12:59,701 --> 00:13:00,399
to justify.

171
00:13:01,100 --> 00:13:05,027
In this model, measurement does not require a conscious observer.

172
00:13:05,027 --> 00:13:10,223
Any physical interaction whose cascade of effects records information about a state in

173
00:13:10,223 --> 00:13:15,600
its environment acts as a query to the server, and yields a definite client-side outcome.

174
00:13:15,600 --> 00:13:21,460
Stated more carefully, client-observable events correspond to measurement outcomes of server-side

175
00:13:21,460 --> 00:13:27,139
quantum evolution. This is the ledger reading of decoherence and of Zurek's quantum Darwinism.

176
00:13:27,840 --> 00:13:30,637
The paper uses an example from Abraham Pais.

177
00:13:30,637 --> 00:13:35,024
Einstein asked whether the Moon exists only when someone looks at it.

178
00:13:35,024 --> 00:13:40,556
The Moon is continuously scattered by sunlight, by cosmic microwave background photons,

179
00:13:40,556 --> 00:13:46,342
and by other particles. So in ledger terms it is queried continuously, and remains definite

180
00:13:46,342 --> 00:13:48,504
whether or not anyone observes it.

181
00:13:49,204 --> 00:13:54,833
The caveat matters. Decoherence accounts for the suppression of observable superpositions,

182
00:13:54,833 --> 00:14:00,212
but it does not by itself select a single outcome, and the paper remains neutral among

183
00:14:00,212 --> 00:14:05,779
interpretations, such as Copenhagen and many-worlds, on how that selection is understood.

184
00:14:05,779 --> 00:14:11,221
A related example is the quantum Zeno effect, observed by Itano and colleagues in 1990:

185
00:14:11,221 --> 00:14:15,850
frequent queries keep re-committing the same state and suppress evolution.

186
00:14:15,850 --> 00:14:21,292
The paper calls this a reinterpretation that leaves the standard calculation unchanged.

187
00:14:21,992 --> 00:14:27,684
The revision adds query-rate limits. Signals and records travel no faster than light.

188
00:14:27,684 --> 00:14:33,778
The Margolus–Levitin bound allows at most two E over pi h-bar orthogonal states per second.

189
00:14:33,778 --> 00:14:39,806
And, as a model assumption with no direct experimental support, each node performs at most

190
00:14:39,806 --> 00:14:44,360
one update per Planck time, about ten to the forty-three per second.

191
00:14:45,060 --> 00:14:48,372
Chapter five. Entanglement as a Shared Record.

192
00:14:49,072 --> 00:14:53,696
Section 5.5 writes the server-side picture as a minimal interface.

193
00:14:53,696 --> 00:14:56,639
A call, entangle, opens one shared record;

194
00:14:56,639 --> 00:15:02,875
each daughter packet carries only a pointer to it, plus its conserved share of energy and

195
00:15:02,875 --> 00:15:07,078
momentum. Relays forward the packets as ordinary link phase.

196
00:15:07,078 --> 00:15:13,314
A call, read, runs on the server: it computes the odds, locks the attribute for the whole

197
00:15:13,314 --> 00:15:18,079
set, and returns plus or minus one. No outcome is stored in advance;

198
00:15:18,079 --> 00:15:22,984
a pre-stored value would be a local hidden variable, capping S at two.

199
00:15:23,683 --> 00:15:30,438
With standard CHSH angles, the shared record gives S equals 2.822 plus or minus 0.007,

200
00:15:30,438 --> 00:15:34,915
against the quantum maximum of two root two, about 2.828.

201
00:15:34,915 --> 00:15:41,826
With eighty percent detector efficiency and one percent dark counts, it gives 2.800 plus

202
00:15:41,826 --> 00:15:48,738
or minus 0.009. A control that gives each daughter its own copied record gives 0.01 plus

203
00:15:48,738 --> 00:15:55,807
or minus 0.01. Each side alone is fifty-fifty, and the near side's statistics do not shift

204
00:15:55,807 --> 00:16:02,718
with the far side's setting. The record is non-local, but no message travels faster than

205
00:16:02,718 --> 00:16:02,875
c.

206
00:16:03,576 --> 00:16:09,073
The caveat is central. The read inserts the cosine-squared odds by hand, so this toy checks

207
00:16:09,073 --> 00:16:13,120
that the interface is consistent; it does not derive the Born rule.

208
00:16:13,120 --> 00:16:18,315
Deriving those odds from the node rules is listed as an open problem, as is how a grid

209
00:16:18,315 --> 00:16:23,087
of local cores carries out the joint read without client-detectable signalling.

210
00:16:23,788 --> 00:16:27,724
Chapter six. Entangled Space and the Information Cap.

211
00:16:28,423 --> 00:16:33,907
Formally, the paper models spacetime as a graph whose vertices are nodes and whose edges

212
00:16:33,907 --> 00:16:39,578
are entanglement bonds. Each node carries a finite-dimensional Hilbert space, and the state

213
00:16:39,578 --> 00:16:44,563
of a region is a network tensor, in the spirit of matrix product states or MERA.

214
00:16:44,563 --> 00:16:48,489
The mathematics is standard quantum mechanics in node language;

215
00:16:48,489 --> 00:16:52,975
the novel content is the interpretation and the discreteness hypothesis.

216
00:16:53,675 --> 00:16:59,873
Now the information cap. The Bekenstein-Hawking entropy of a black hole is A over four

217
00:16:59,873 --> 00:17:06,215
l P squared, in natural units. Converted to bits, that is about one bit per four ln two,

218
00:17:06,215 --> 00:17:10,684
roughly 2.77 Planck areas, or about 0.36 bits per Planck area.

219
00:17:10,684 --> 00:17:16,882
Under the holographic principle, no region can hold more information than a black hole

220
00:17:16,882 --> 00:17:18,755
of the same boundary area.

221
00:17:19,455 --> 00:17:24,308
The paper applies this to cubic Planck nodes carrying one qubit each.

222
00:17:24,308 --> 00:17:30,708
A single cube has six faces of one Planck area, so its cap is about 2.2 bits, and one qubit

223
00:17:30,708 --> 00:17:37,038
fits. But for a block N nodes on a side, independent qubits would give N cubed bits, while

224
00:17:37,038 --> 00:17:40,132
the surface cap is only about 2.2 N squared.

225
00:17:40,132 --> 00:17:44,703
The volume count exceeds the cap once N is larger than about 2.2.

226
00:17:45,403 --> 00:17:51,895
So inside a block N nodes wide, each node can carry at most about 2.2 over N independent

227
00:17:51,895 --> 00:17:58,238
bits: about 0.2 for N equals ten, and about ten to the minus twenty for a proton-sized

228
00:17:58,238 --> 00:18:01,483
region, where N is around ten to the twenty.

229
00:18:02,183 --> 00:18:07,910
The interpretation is that each node can still hold a qubit, but node states within a region

230
00:18:07,910 --> 00:18:13,263
cannot all be independent. Nearly all must be entangled with one another or determined

231
00:18:13,263 --> 00:18:18,679
by the boundary, so free degrees of freedom scale with surface area rather than volume.

232
00:18:18,679 --> 00:18:24,156
In ledger terms, the server stores a region largely through its boundary, with a tightly

233
00:18:24,156 --> 00:18:29,883
correlated interior. This is consistent with holography and with proposals, by Van Raamsdonk

234
00:18:29,883 --> 00:18:35,112
and by Maldacena and Susskind, that spacetime connectivity arises from entanglement.

235
00:18:35,811 --> 00:18:41,372
The caveats: the argument assumes the holographic bound applies to the lattice, which has

236
00:18:41,372 --> 00:18:47,121
not been proven for a discrete network. A simple cubic lattice is anisotropic, so the factor

237
00:18:47,121 --> 00:18:52,244
2.2 depends on node shape, although the N squared versus N cubed scaling does not.

238
00:18:52,244 --> 00:18:57,555
And the model does not yet supply an update rule that produces area-law entanglement.

239
00:18:58,255 --> 00:19:01,639
Chapter seven. Higgs Field versus Higgs Boson.

240
00:19:02,339 --> 00:19:09,405
In ledger language, the Higgs vacuum value, v, about 246 GeV, is a property of the network's

241
00:19:09,405 --> 00:19:12,861
ground state, held server-side at every node.

242
00:19:12,861 --> 00:19:19,466
The Higgs boson, observed at the LHC in 2012 and now measured at about 125.2 GeV, is a

243
00:19:19,466 --> 00:19:24,611
client-side excitation of that state: a ripple on the ground state.

244
00:19:24,611 --> 00:19:27,760
The measured values are standard physics;

245
00:19:27,760 --> 00:19:31,523
the server and client assignment is the proposal.

246
00:19:32,223 --> 00:19:38,000
At tree level, the Higgs mass equals the square root of two lambda, times v.

247
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:43,319
That gives lambda of about 0.13, and a ratio m H over v of about 0.51.

248
00:19:43,319 --> 00:19:49,855
The paper reads this ratio as a measure of how stiff the ground state is against small

249
00:19:49,855 --> 00:19:50,919
displacements.

250
00:19:51,619 --> 00:19:56,885
The hierarchy problem: radiative corrections to the Higgs mass parameter grow with the

251
00:19:56,885 --> 00:20:02,395
square of the cutoff, while the Higgs mass over the Planck mass is around ten to the minus

252
00:20:02,395 --> 00:20:07,538
seventeen. A discrete network supplies a physical cutoff, so corrections are finite.

253
00:20:07,538 --> 00:20:13,170
But the paper is clear that this is shared by any lattice regularization and does not remove

254
00:20:13,170 --> 00:20:18,435
the fine-tuning. The model offers no explanation of this hierarchy, or of the weakness

255
00:20:18,435 --> 00:20:19,843
of gravity, at present.

256
00:20:20,543 --> 00:20:24,234
There is published precedent for near-criticality.

257
00:20:24,234 --> 00:20:30,656
Froggatt and Nielsen in 1996 proposed two degenerate minima of the effective potential,

258
00:20:30,656 --> 00:20:37,300
one near the Planck scale, and predicted a top mass of 173 plus or minus 5 GeV and a Higgs

259
00:20:37,300 --> 00:20:44,239
mass of 135 plus or minus 9 GeV. Shaposhnikov and Wetterich in 2010 argued that asymptotically

260
00:20:44,239 --> 00:20:50,366
safe gravity drives lambda toward zero near the Planck scale, giving about 126 GeV.

261
00:20:50,366 --> 00:20:56,862
Buttazzo and colleagues in 2013 showed the measured parameters place the vacuum close to

262
00:20:56,862 --> 00:20:58,559
the stability boundary.

263
00:20:59,259 --> 00:21:05,947
The paper reports an independent rerun using two-loop Standard Model running with Buttazzo

264
00:21:05,947 --> 00:21:12,337
matching conditions. Imposing lambda at the Planck mass equals zero gives a Higgs mass

265
00:21:12,337 --> 00:21:18,653
of about 128.8 GeV, against the measured 125.2, agreement within about three percent.

266
00:21:18,653 --> 00:21:25,191
Exact agreement would need a top mass near 171 GeV versus a measured 172.6, with roughly

267
00:21:25,191 --> 00:21:30,021
one GeV of interpretive uncertainty in the pole-mass translation.

268
00:21:30,021 --> 00:21:37,080
With current inputs, lambda reaches a minimum of about minus 0.011 between ten to the seventeen

269
00:21:37,080 --> 00:21:43,395
and ten to the nineteen GeV: the vacuum sits near a critical point and is metastable.

270
00:21:44,095 --> 00:21:49,048
In ledger terms, this is a candidate statement that the network's ground state sits near

271
00:21:49,048 --> 00:21:51,581
a critical point. It is not yet a derivation.

272
00:21:51,581 --> 00:21:56,477
And the paper notes that the near-zero lambda at the Planck scale is the strongest part

273
00:21:56,477 --> 00:22:01,374
of this discussion, but it was established in the earlier literature, not by the ledger

274
00:22:01,374 --> 00:22:01,711
model.

275
00:22:02,411 --> 00:22:04,619
Chapter eight. A Toy Lattice.

276
00:22:05,319 --> 00:22:11,017
To see what near-criticality means on a discrete substrate, the paper studies a four-dimensional

277
00:22:11,017 --> 00:22:16,477
hypercubic lattice at Planck spacing, with eight nearest neighbours per node and one complex

278
00:22:16,477 --> 00:22:22,056
doublet per node. The doublet has four real components, the same content as the Higgs doublet,

279
00:22:22,056 --> 00:22:24,786
and matching a qubit's two complex amplitudes.

280
00:22:24,786 --> 00:22:29,415
The action has a hopping parameter kappa and a lattice self-coupling lambda L.

281
00:22:30,115 --> 00:22:36,160
In the free theory, the critical point is exactly kappa c equals one eighth, the inverse

282
00:22:36,160 --> 00:22:42,342
of the number of neighbours. This mean-field figure from the study shows m H over v versus

283
00:22:42,342 --> 00:22:48,524
kappa for several couplings, with kappa c equals one eighth marked, the value 0.49 marked,

284
00:22:48,524 --> 00:22:50,515
and the target ratio of 0.51.

285
00:22:51,215 --> 00:22:58,925
Monte Carlo runs on lattices of size eight, twelve and sixteen locate the critical point

286
00:22:58,925 --> 00:23:04,707
from Binder-cumulant crossings. Self-interaction shifts it upward:

287
00:23:04,707 --> 00:23:10,051
kappa c of 0.1259 plus or minus 0.0005 for lambda L of 0.001;

288
00:23:10,051 --> 00:23:16,972
0.1288 plus or minus 0.0009 for 0.01; and 0.1442 plus or minus 0.0014 for 0.05.

289
00:23:16,972 --> 00:23:23,280
In this figure from the paper, the Binder curves cross above one eighth.

290
00:23:23,280 --> 00:23:31,340
Mean-field theory places the critical point below one eighth for these couplings, and misses

291
00:23:31,340 --> 00:23:32,303
this shift.

292
00:23:33,003 --> 00:23:38,663
Just past criticality, the measured vacuum value follows v squared proportional to kappa

293
00:23:38,663 --> 00:23:41,814
minus kappa c, as expected near a critical point.

294
00:23:41,814 --> 00:23:47,344
This figure shows the order parameter versus distance past the critical point, and the

295
00:23:47,344 --> 00:23:49,659
Monte Carlo estimates of m H over v.

296
00:23:50,359 --> 00:23:53,439
The revision adds a toy-tested hypothesis:

297
00:23:53,439 --> 00:23:59,744
self-tuning the knob by homing. A local rule can bring kappa near kappa c without hand

298
00:23:59,744 --> 00:24:04,802
tuning, if it has a signal that peaks there, here an anisotropy cost.

299
00:24:04,802 --> 00:24:11,107
Like CNC homing to a limit switch, the knob is dithered, steps at full rate toward the

300
00:24:11,107 --> 00:24:15,800
better side, and shrinks its step only on a confirmed sign flip.

301
00:24:15,800 --> 00:24:22,105
On an O four toy on an eight-link BCC grid, with kappa c about 0.1495, all twelve runs

302
00:24:22,105 --> 00:24:26,503
locked, from hot and cold starts, about 0.006 above kappa c.

303
00:24:27,203 --> 00:24:33,097
Here is the sobering number. To obtain v equals 246 GeV with Planck spacing requires kappa

304
00:24:33,097 --> 00:24:36,503
minus kappa c of order ten to the minus thirty-four.

305
00:24:36,503 --> 00:24:42,200
The paper is explicit that this is the hierarchy problem restated on the lattice, not a

306
00:24:42,200 --> 00:24:48,291
solution. In the pure Standard Model, radiative symmetry breaking does not supply the offset,

307
00:24:48,291 --> 00:24:52,547
because the top quark contribution dominates with the wrong sign.

308
00:24:53,247 --> 00:24:58,302
The caveats are substantial. The lattices are small and the error bars sizable.

309
00:24:58,302 --> 00:25:01,374
Gauge fields and the top quark are not included.

310
00:25:01,374 --> 00:25:05,213
A hypercubic lattice breaks rotational and Lorentz symmetry.

311
00:25:05,213 --> 00:25:10,971
Mean-field and Monte Carlo disagree on the direction of the shift, and the conversion from

312
00:25:10,971 --> 00:25:16,602
lattice units to GeV is schematic. For the homing rule, the residual bias of about 0.006

313
00:25:16,602 --> 00:25:22,297
does not shrink from lattice size eight to twelve, the rule still has hand-set constants,

314
00:25:22,297 --> 00:25:28,311
and getting from ten to the minus three down to ten to the minus thirty-four is not addressed.

315
00:25:29,011 --> 00:25:31,939
Chapter nine. Steps Toward Gravity.

316
00:25:32,639 --> 00:25:36,145
None of what follows is derived from a node rule yet;

317
00:25:36,145 --> 00:25:39,188
these are candidate paths and worked examples.

318
00:25:39,188 --> 00:25:42,628
The nodes are rigid. Mass-energy does not bend them;

319
00:25:42,628 --> 00:25:48,516
it changes the workload of nearby nodes, and so the cost of routing a signal and the rate

320
00:25:48,516 --> 00:25:54,403
of local clocks. In weak-field general relativity, the metric can be read as a refractive

321
00:25:54,403 --> 00:26:00,158
index, n of r, about one plus two G M over r c squared, an optical-metric picture going

322
00:26:00,158 --> 00:26:01,679
back to Gordon in 1923.

323
00:26:02,379 --> 00:26:08,731
The key identity: G M over r c squared equals mass in Planck masses times inverse distance

324
00:26:08,731 --> 00:26:14,801
in Planck lengths. The Sun holds about 3.3 times ten to the 131 Planck-scale nodes, so

325
00:26:14,801 --> 00:26:21,082
its mean workload is about 2.7 times ten to the minus ninety-four Planck masses per node.

326
00:26:21,082 --> 00:26:24,963
Each share is tiny; the effect comes only from the sum.

327
00:26:25,663 --> 00:26:31,873
The Sun was coarse-grained into up to about a million cells, with masses following Eddington's

328
00:26:31,873 --> 00:26:37,554
standard polytrope model, and the deflection of a ray grazing the limb was summed cell

329
00:26:37,554 --> 00:26:43,235
by cell. It converges to four G M over R c squared, 1.751 arc seconds, with a relative

330
00:26:43,235 --> 00:26:46,010
difference of about ten to the minus nine.

331
00:26:46,010 --> 00:26:49,974
The paper is explicit that this is expected by construction.

332
00:26:49,974 --> 00:26:56,183
The factor of two, equal time and space contributions, is still to be derived from an explicit

333
00:26:56,183 --> 00:26:58,759
node rule; that is open problem twenty.

334
00:26:59,459 --> 00:27:04,324
Section 6.14 applies the same node-summed solar field to Mercury's orbit.

335
00:27:04,324 --> 00:27:10,587
This figure shows the perihelion direction drifting over two hundred orbits, and the simulated

336
00:27:10,587 --> 00:27:16,251
advance against the post-Newtonian factor, two plus two gamma minus beta, over three;

337
00:27:16,251 --> 00:27:20,715
the dashed line is 42.98 arc seconds per century times that factor.

338
00:27:20,715 --> 00:27:26,579
The paper's caveat for this figure: gamma and beta were borrowed from the post-Newtonian

339
00:27:26,579 --> 00:27:30,443
equations of general relativity and inserted, not derived.

340
00:27:31,143 --> 00:27:34,273
Section 6.18 goes further, without a figure.

341
00:27:34,273 --> 00:27:40,602
If a node's clock rate is relayed between neighbours, and each hop compounds the slowdown

342
00:27:40,602 --> 00:27:47,002
rather than adding to it, the time part of the metric becomes e to the minus two U, giving

343
00:27:47,002 --> 00:27:53,473
beta equal to one. In simulation, the compounding relay gave 42.91 arc seconds per century,

344
00:27:53,473 --> 00:27:59,234
against about 43 observed. An additive relay gave 50.1, which the paper excludes.

345
00:27:59,234 --> 00:28:05,349
A strictly neighbour-relayed clock produced compounding without it being imposed, with

346
00:28:05,349 --> 00:28:11,607
beta reaching 0.9992 on the finest grid. Gamma equals one is still assumed, not derived.

347
00:28:12,307 --> 00:28:18,543
A rigid grid defines a rest frame. Following the Lorentz–Poincaré reading presented by

348
00:28:18,543 --> 00:28:23,111
Bell, a preferred server frame may exist yet stay undetectable.

349
00:28:23,111 --> 00:28:29,636
Two toys test this. Waves on a cubic lattice slow by a direction-dependent amount of order

350
00:28:29,636 --> 00:28:35,872
k a squared. And here, sine-Gordon kinks re-plotted on fixed nodes contract by exactly

351
00:28:35,872 --> 00:28:42,107
one over gamma, with energy gamma m within 0.04 percent up to 0.95 c, while much wider

352
00:28:42,107 --> 00:28:46,747
than the spacing. Near the spacing, they radiate, slow, and pin.

353
00:28:47,447 --> 00:28:53,007
The conclusion, for these update rules: direction dependence and departures from Lorentz

354
00:28:53,007 --> 00:28:58,124
contraction both fall off as the pattern size over the spacing, to the minus two.

355
00:28:58,124 --> 00:29:03,368
That yields a new constraint: physical particles must be much larger than one node.

356
00:29:03,368 --> 00:29:09,370
A proton spans about five times ten to the nineteen Planck lengths, so for accessible particles

357
00:29:09,370 --> 00:29:12,529
the effect is below about ten to the minus thirty.

358
00:29:12,529 --> 00:29:18,278
The paper flags the remaining challenge: radiative corrections in virtual loops could carry

359
00:29:18,278 --> 00:29:20,615
grid anisotropy down to low energies.

360
00:29:21,315 --> 00:29:24,723
Chapter ten. Black Holes Without Singularities.

361
00:29:25,423 --> 00:29:31,066
Far from the core, the network is assumed to reduce to general relativity, so horizons,

362
00:29:31,066 --> 00:29:35,994
Event Horizon Telescope shadows, and LIGO and Virgo ringdowns are unchanged.

363
00:29:35,994 --> 00:29:41,961
Inside, with a minimum node size, curvature is bounded at roughly one over the Planck length

364
00:29:41,961 --> 00:29:47,797
squared, and density at roughly the Planck density, about five times ten to the ninety-six

365
00:29:47,797 --> 00:29:53,958
kilograms per cubic metre. The singularity is a candidate to be replaced by a finite, maximally

366
00:29:53,958 --> 00:29:59,600
loaded core. Precedents include the big bounce of loop quantum cosmology, regular black

367
00:29:59,600 --> 00:30:04,399
holes of Bardeen and Hayward, Planck stars, and Markov's limiting density.

368
00:30:05,099 --> 00:30:10,995
Section 6.17 turns this into a rough numerical estimate, using a placeholder rule that

369
00:30:10,995 --> 00:30:14,286
no node carries more than about one Planck mass.

370
00:30:14,286 --> 00:30:18,468
A mass M then cannot be packed below a minimum radius, R min.

371
00:30:18,468 --> 00:30:24,707
General relativity's Kretschmann curvature grows as one over R to the sixth, without limit.

372
00:30:24,707 --> 00:30:30,602
With the cap it levels off at about 842 over the Planck length to the fourth, the same

373
00:30:30,602 --> 00:30:36,155
for every mass, because the saturated density is always about the Planck density.

374
00:30:36,855 --> 00:30:42,436
This figure shows the levelling-off for the Sun, one kilogram, and one Planck mass.

375
00:30:42,436 --> 00:30:48,421
The saturated radius is about 4.5 times ten to the minus twenty-three metres for the Sun,

376
00:30:48,421 --> 00:30:54,876
far inside its three-kilometre horizon. For the Sun as it is, the change from general relativity

377
00:30:54,876 --> 00:31:00,658
is around ten to the minus ninety-three, so the light-bending and Mercury examples are

378
00:31:00,658 --> 00:31:06,508
unchanged. In this toy, a horizon forms only above about 0.17 Planck masses, about four

379
00:31:06,508 --> 00:31:09,063
times ten to the minus nine kilograms.

380
00:31:09,763 --> 00:31:14,702
The caveats: the cap value is a placeholder, not derived from the node rules;

381
00:31:14,702 --> 00:31:17,588
the minimum black-hole mass is toy-dependent;

382
00:31:17,588 --> 00:31:23,425
and the fate of a fully loaded core, including bounce or white-hole outcomes, is left open,

383
00:31:23,425 --> 00:31:28,363
as is how information escapes in Hawking radiation, following the Page curve.

384
00:31:29,063 --> 00:31:31,727
Chapter eleven. Testable Predictions.

385
00:31:32,427 --> 00:31:36,731
A framework that only restates quantum mechanics cannot be tested.

386
00:31:36,731 --> 00:31:42,014
The ledger's distinct physical claim is discreteness at roughly the Planck scale.

387
00:31:42,014 --> 00:31:47,752
On a regular lattice, high-energy waves feel the grid, and a generic linear modification

388
00:31:47,752 --> 00:31:53,556
makes photon speed depend on energy. Two photons emitted together a distance D away, with

389
00:31:53,556 --> 00:31:59,164
energy difference delta E, would arrive separated by delta t of about xi times delta E

390
00:31:59,164 --> 00:32:01,707
over the Planck energy, times D over c.

391
00:32:02,407 --> 00:32:10,439
Observations already constrain this. From GRB 090510, Fermi found no evidence of Lorentz-invariance

392
00:32:10,439 --> 00:32:17,172
violation, with a lower limit above 1.2 times the Planck energy on a linear effect.

393
00:32:17,172 --> 00:32:24,311
From GRB 221009A, the brightest burst ever recorded, LHAASO set limits around ten to the

394
00:32:24,311 --> 00:32:28,205
twenty GeV, roughly ten times the Planck energy.

395
00:32:28,205 --> 00:32:34,289
A naive regular grid with an order-one coefficient is therefore disfavored;

396
00:32:34,289 --> 00:32:37,615
the data require xi of about 0.1 or less.

397
00:32:38,315 --> 00:32:41,422
The revised paper's response has two parts.

398
00:32:41,422 --> 00:32:45,611
First, the lattice rule it tested produces no linear term;

399
00:32:45,611 --> 00:32:52,040
its leading effect is quadratic. At Planck spacing, the fractional speed shortfall is one

400
00:32:52,040 --> 00:32:57,096
eighth of E over the Planck energy, squared, times a direction factor.

401
00:32:57,096 --> 00:33:03,308
For a 31 GeV photon, like the highest-energy photon from GRB 090510, that is about 8.1

402
00:33:03,308 --> 00:33:06,920
times ten to the minus thirty-seven along an axis.

403
00:33:06,920 --> 00:33:13,854
Second, a random, causal-set-like network remains a fallback, under which first-order dispersion

404
00:33:13,854 --> 00:33:15,443
is expected to vanish.

405
00:33:16,143 --> 00:33:22,165
The revision names an accessible test: gravity-mediated entanglement, as proposed by Bose

406
00:33:22,165 --> 00:33:25,548
and colleagues and by Marletto and Vedral in 2017.

407
00:33:25,548 --> 00:33:31,637
Two small masses, each in a superposition of two locations, interact only through gravity.

408
00:33:31,637 --> 00:33:37,793
In the ledger, each branch loads nodes differently, so the expectation is that entanglement

409
00:33:37,793 --> 00:33:43,950
would be observed. A null result, with a working experiment, would count against the model.

410
00:33:43,950 --> 00:33:50,242
The caveat: this is an expectation, not a derivation, and it matches other quantum treatments

411
00:33:50,242 --> 00:33:54,639
of gravity, so a positive result would not single out this model.

412
00:33:55,339 --> 00:33:59,720
Relativistic effects of laboratory-scale sources have not been measured.

413
00:33:59,720 --> 00:34:02,398
The paper estimates fractional clock shifts:

414
00:34:02,398 --> 00:34:07,691
about 3.2 times ten to the minus twenty-four near a one-tonne tungsten block, and about

415
00:34:07,691 --> 00:34:12,924
four times ten to the minus twenty at the apex of the Great Pyramid from its own mass.

416
00:34:12,924 --> 00:34:18,035
Both lie below the roughly ten to the minus eighteen reached by today's best clocks.

417
00:34:18,035 --> 00:34:23,390
The node sum reproduces general relativity here, so such a test would check consistency,

418
00:34:23,390 --> 00:34:24,667
not separate the two.

419
00:34:25,367 --> 00:34:31,079
Two more commitments. One update per Planck time gives a clock rate of about 1.85 times

420
00:34:31,079 --> 00:34:36,790
ten to the forty-three hertz, and a ceiling near nine times ten to the forty-two hertz,

421
00:34:36,790 --> 00:34:42,238
energies of order the Planck energy. No physical wave or particle should exceed it.

422
00:34:42,238 --> 00:34:48,081
And the flat per-node vacuum cost predicts dark energy with w equal to minus one exactly.

423
00:34:48,081 --> 00:34:54,186
The paper states that a confirmed time-varying dark energy, as hinted by recent DESI results,

424
00:34:54,186 --> 00:34:55,367
would rule it out.

425
00:34:56,067 --> 00:34:58,467
Chapter twelve. Open Problems.

426
00:34:59,167 --> 00:35:02,325
The list of open problems has grown to twenty-seven.

427
00:35:02,325 --> 00:35:07,912
Among the most central: derive the Born read-out rule rather than postulate it, now narrowed

428
00:35:07,912 --> 00:35:11,556
to why the ledger carries a complex Hilbert-space structure;

429
00:35:11,556 --> 00:35:17,082
specify a concrete network tensor and update rule that reproduce relativistic quantum field

430
00:35:17,082 --> 00:35:21,940
theory; show that server-side ordering of updates stays unobservable to clients;

431
00:35:21,940 --> 00:35:27,466
and make the mass-loads-the-server idea quantitative, recovering Einstein's field equations

432
00:35:27,466 --> 00:35:29,287
and the equivalence principle.

433
00:35:29,987 --> 00:35:35,547
New items include: deriving the factor of two in light bending, and beta and gamma for

434
00:35:35,547 --> 00:35:41,042
Mercury, from one node rule; deriving the saturation cap and minimum black-hole mass;

435
00:35:41,042 --> 00:35:44,015
coupling chiral modes to the weak gauge field;

436
00:35:44,015 --> 00:35:47,765
and deriving the cosine-squared odds of the shared record.

437
00:35:47,765 --> 00:35:53,583
Section 8 adds framework objectives: a Born rule from local projection, Lorentz invariance

438
00:35:53,583 --> 00:35:58,043
under radiative corrections, and an anomaly-free chiral gauge sector.

439
00:35:58,743 --> 00:36:01,395
The paper closes with a methodological note.

440
00:36:01,395 --> 00:36:06,819
To stay falsifiable, server-side explanations are reserved for regimes where known physics

441
00:36:06,819 --> 00:36:12,604
fails or is silent: quantum correlations and measurement, Planck-scale structure, and black-hole

442
00:36:12,604 --> 00:36:18,027
interiors. Where established client-side physics already explains a phenomenon, the ledger

443
00:36:18,027 --> 00:36:23,632
reinterprets it but does not replace it. As the paper puts it, a framework that can attribute

444
00:36:23,632 --> 00:36:26,343
anything to the server would predict nothing.

445
00:36:27,043 --> 00:36:31,226
To summarize. The Space-Ledger is a potential candidate hypothesis:

446
00:36:31,226 --> 00:36:36,845
spacetime as a discrete, entangled network that holds the quantum state, with non-locality

447
00:36:36,845 --> 00:36:40,965
located server-side and a local, light-speed-limited client world.

448
00:36:40,965 --> 00:36:47,083
The revised paper adds a finiteness principle, toy checks of read-out rules and of a shared-record

449
00:36:47,083 --> 00:36:53,201
Bell test, worked examples toward gravity, a finite black-hole core, and new testable commitments.

450
00:36:53,201 --> 00:36:58,757
Much of it reorganizes standard physics; its distinct claims are discreteness, a read-out

451
00:36:58,757 --> 00:37:01,379
postulate, and those concrete commitments.

452
00:37:01,379 --> 00:37:05,624
Whether it survives depends on the open problems and on future data.

453
00:37:05,624 --> 00:37:08,683
The paper is by Edmond Ying. Thanks for watching.
